WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.300
Last, the radio, which is a navigation tool as much as a safety one.

2
00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.600
VHF range is roughly line of sight.

3
00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.925
So what gives you more range: more power, or a higher antenna?

4
00:00:13.925 --> 00:00:15.750
The higher antenna.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.975
Now the channels to know.

6
00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:21.450
Sixteen is distress, safety and calling.

7
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.925
Nine is the alternate calling channel for recreational boats.

8
00:00:26.175 --> 00:00:30.050
Thirteen is bridge-to-bridge, where the ships and tugs talk.

9
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.175
And twenty-two alpha is the Coast Guard’s working channel.

10
00:00:34.425 --> 00:00:43.425
For the DSC distress button to do its job, the radio needs two things: a registered MMSI number, and a GPS connection.

11
00:00:43.675 --> 00:00:48.425
And the weather channels carry NOAA Weather Radio around the clock.

12
00:00:48.675 --> 00:00:50.650
That’s lesson eleven.

13
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.450
GPS course comes from movement.

14
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.750
Check every leg of a route.

15
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.075
AIS isn’t in sight.

16
00:00:58.325 --> 00:01:00.750
Know what your depth reading means.

17
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.150
Next time: radar.
